.TH BT-INTERCOM 1
.SH NAME
bt-intercom \- half-duplex or full-duplex Bluetooth headset intercom
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt-intercom
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bt-intercom
routes configured Bluetooth Classic headsets using BlueZ, PipeWire, and
WirePlumber. When talk groups are configured, a microphone is routed only to
other members of its groups; otherwise all headsets share the intercom. Run it
as the same user that owns the PipeWire session; do not run it with
.BR sudo .
The program does not change headset profiles or modify PipeWire links it does
not own.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Scan for nearby Bluetooth devices.
.TP
.BI pair " ADDRESS"
Pair, trust, and connect a headset. Pairing requires an interactive terminal.
.TP
.BI remove " ADDRESS"
Remove a headset from the saved intercom network. Bluetooth pairing is unchanged;
restart a running intercom or service for the change to take effect. Removing
the last address clears the saved network.
.TP
.BI status " ADDRESS" ...
Show each headset's Bluetooth name, duplex audio availability, and port counts.
.TP
.BI run " [ADDRESS ...]"
Run the intercom. Explicit addresses are saved for future runs; if omitted, the
previously saved network is restored.
.TP
.BI completions " SHELL"
Write shell completion definitions to standard output. Supported shells include
Bash, Zsh, Fish, and Elvish.
.TP
.B tui
Open the Ratatui terminal control panel to monitor headsets and edit talk
groups. Use Tab and arrow keys to navigate; press n to add a group, d to remove
the selected group, and Space to toggle headset membership.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI scan " --seconds SECONDS"
Set the scan duration from 1 through 300 seconds (default: 15).
.TP
.BI run " --interval SECONDS"
Set the routing update interval (default: 2 seconds).
.TP
.B run --connect
Retry disconnected headsets every 30 seconds.
.TP
.BI run " --mode MODE"
Select full-duplex (default) or half-duplex. Half-duplex requires a play/pause
input mapping for every headset and grants one microphone at a time.
.TP
.BI run " --ptt ADDRESS=/dev/input/eventX"
Map a headset to its Linux evdev push-to-talk input. Repeat once for every
configured headset. Only the headset's play/pause key press and release events
are used.
.PP
Hold play/pause to talk in PTT mode; release to mute. In half-duplex, requests
wait in first-in, first-out order while another headset transmits. Keep holding
until a double beep confirms an active microphone route; releasing cancels a
queued request or ends the current turn. Each talk group has an independent
queue and can have one active talker. A headset in multiple groups can hold the
floor in each; with no groups configured, all headsets share one queue.
Half-duplex routing failures stop the run and release owned links. External
links remain untouched; exclusivity applies only to this process's routes.
.PP
In full-duplex, mapped headsets start in PTT mode. Press play/pause three times
within one second to toggle that headset independently between PTT and
always-open. Choices reset when the process exits or that headset loses duplex
audio. Without mappings, full-duplex starts with all microphones open.
Losing duplex audio resets a mapped headset's request and always-open choice;
release and press again after reconnecting.
.TP
.B run --dashboard
Show the live terminal dashboard on standard error. Requires an interactive
terminal.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show command help.
.TP
.B -V
Show the program version.
.TP
.B --version
Show the program version and the UTC build datetime.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The saved headset network is stored at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bt-intercom/headsets ,
or
.IR ~/.config/bt-intercom/headsets
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or not absolute. Temporary PTT confirmation audio uses
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
when it is absolute and otherwise uses the system temporary directory.
.PP
Talk groups are stored in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bt-intercom/talk-groups.json ,
or
.IR ~/.config/bt-intercom/talk-groups.json
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or not absolute. A running intercom reloads group changes on the next
routing update.
.PP
Runtime log levels are controlled by
.BR RUST_LOG .
The default level is
.BR info ;
for example, set
.B RUST_LOG=warn
to show warnings and errors only. Logs are written to standard error and are
captured by the systemd journal when run as a user service.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command exits with status 0 on success, 1 for runtime or semantic
configuration failures, and 2 for command-line syntax errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bluetoothctl (1),
.BR pw-cli (1),
.BR pw-dump (1),
.BR pw-play (1)
